.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt EXABGP.CONF 5
.OS
.Sh NAME
.Nm exabgp.conf
.Nd Configuration file controlling the BGP configuration for
.Xr exabgp 1 .
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
specifies the BGP neighbor configuration for exabgp.
.Ss CONFIGURATION FILE SYNTAX
.Pp
.Bd -literal -offset 3m
.\" syntax: generated by qa/bin/update_man_syntax, do not edit
process <name> {  # an external program exabgp runs and talks to over the API
    # the program to run, with its arguments, mandatory
    run <program> [<argument> ...];
    # how messages to the program are written, default text
    encoder text|json;
    respawn true|false;  # restart the program when it exits, default true
    # what happens to the routes the program announced when it exits; unset,
    # they are kept for a program using API 4 and withdrawn otherwise
    on-exit withdraw|keep;
}
neighbor <ip>[/<mask>] {  # a BGP peer
    peer-address <ip>[/<mask>];  # the peer, or the peers of a range
    local-address <ip>|auto;  # the address to connect from, auto to find it
    local-link-local <ip>;  # the IPv6 link-local address (fe80::/10)
    local-as <asn>|auto;  # our AS, auto to use the peer AS
    peer-as <asn>|auto;  # the peer AS, auto to use ours
    router-id <ipv4>;  # the BGP identifier, the local address by default
    description <description>;  # free text about the neighbor
    host-name <host-name>;  # sent in the hostname capability
    domain-name <domain-name>;  # sent in the hostname capability
    hold-time 0|<3-65535>;  # seconds, 0 disables the hold timer
    rate-limit <number>;  # UPDATE messages per second
    passive true|false;  # wait for the peer to connect
    listen <1-65535>;  # the port to listen on
    connect <1-65535>;  # the port to connect to
    # the interface the session is bound to
    source-interface <source-interface>;
    # the TTL of the packets sent, for a multihop session or GTSM
    outgoing-ttl <0-255>|disable;
    incoming-ttl <0-255>|disable;  # the lowest TTL accepted (GTSM)
    md5-password <md5-password>;  # the TCP MD5 signature key, RFC 2385
    md5-base64 true|false;  # the md5-password is base64 encoded
    # the local address the TCP MD5 key is set on, the local-address by
    # default
    md5-ip <ip>;
    # RFC 9774, what to do with a route with an AS_SET
    as-set withdraw|accept;
    # RFC 9012 11, a received Tunnel Encapsulation attribute: auto filters it
    # on EBGP only
    tunnel-encapsulation auto|filter|accept;
    # RFC 8955 6, hold back a received flow with no matching unicast route;
    # relaxed accepts one with no destination
    flow-validation disable|enable|relaxed;
    # RFC 8955 6, withdraw an EBGP route whose AS_PATH does not start with the
    # peer AS
    enforce-first-as true|false;
    # RFC 4684 5, send VPN routes only for the Route Targets the peer is a
    # member of
    route-target-filter true|false;
    # send routes with the same attributes in one UPDATE
    group-updates true|false;
    # send the routes an API command changes without waiting for a flush
    auto-flush true|false;
    # keep the routes sent, to send them again on a route refresh or a new
    # session
    adj-rib-out true|false;
    adj-rib-in true|false;  # keep the routes received
    # send the End-of-RIB markers only when the API asks for them
    manual-eor true|false;
    shutdown true|false;  # start with the session administratively down
    # the templates whose statements the neighbor takes
    inherit <template>|[ <template> ... ];
    family {  # the address families to negotiate
        # an ipv4 family to negotiate, may be repeated
        ipv4 unicast|multicast|nlri-mpls|labeled-unicast|mpls-vpn|mcast-vpn|
                flow|flow-vpn|mup|sr-policy|rtc [prefix-limit <n>];
        # an ipv6 family to negotiate, may be repeated
        ipv6 unicast|multicast|nlri-mpls|labeled-unicast|mpls-vpn|mcast-vpn|
                mup|sr-policy|flow|flow-vpn [prefix-limit <n>];
        # an l2vpn family to negotiate, may be repeated
        l2vpn vpls|evpn [prefix-limit <n>];
        # an bgp-ls family to negotiate, may be repeated
        bgp-ls bgp-ls|bgp-ls-vpn [prefix-limit <n>];
        all;  # every family exabgp knows, may be repeated
    }
    capability {  # the capabilities to negotiate
        # Extended Next Hop Encoding, RFC 8950
        nexthop enable|disable|require;
        # ADD-PATH, RFC 7911: receive, send or both
        add-path disable|receive|send|send/receive;
        asn4 enable|disable|require;  # four-octet AS numbers, RFC 6793
        # Graceful Restart, RFC 4724: the restart time in seconds, 0 for the
        # hold time
        graceful-restart <0-4095>|disable;
        # Multisession, draft-ietf-idr-bgp-multisession: a session per family
        multi-session true|false;
        # Operational messages, draft-ietf-idr-operational-message
        operational enable|disable|require;
        # Route Refresh, RFC 2918, and Enhanced Route Refresh, RFC 7313, both
        route-refresh enable|disable|require;
        # Route Refresh, RFC 2918, alone: what route-refresh says of it,
        # overridden
        route-refresh-normal enable|disable|require;
        # Enhanced Route Refresh, RFC 7313, alone: what route-refresh says of
        # it, overridden
        route-refresh-enhanced enable|disable|require;
        aigp true|false;  # accept and send the AIGP attribute, RFC 7311
        # Extended Messages, RFC 8654: messages up to 65535 octets
        extended-message enable|disable|require;
        # Software Version, draft-ietf-idr-software-version
        software-version enable|disable|require;
        # Link-Local Next Hop, draft-ietf-idr-linklocal-capability
        link-local-nexthop enable|disable|require;
        # Multiple Labels, RFC 8277: how many labels on one prefix we take,
        # per labelled family
        multiple-labels <2-255>|disable;
        # use the link-local IPv6 next-hop when a route has both
        link-local-prefer true|false;
    }
    tcp-ao {  # TCP-AO (RFC 5925) authentication
        keyid <0-255>;  # the key identifier
        # the MAC algorithm, RFC 5926
        algorithm hmac-sha-1-96|aes-128-cmac-96|hmac-sha-256;
        password <password>;  # the master key
        base64 true|false;  # the password is base64 encoded
    }
    role {  # the RFC 9234 role of this router on the session
        local provider|rs|rs-client|customer|peer;  # our role on the session
        strict enable|disable;  # refuse a peer which does not send its role
        # give the roles to the API programs with the routes
        add-meta enable|disable;
        otc;
    }
    confederation {  # RFC 5065 BGP confederation
        # the AS Confederation Identifier, the AS the world outside sees
        identifier <asn>;
        # the Member-AS numbers of the confederation, but our own
        members <asn>|[ <asn> ... ];
    }
    # the families ADD-PATH is negotiated for, with an optional PATHS-LIMIT
    add-path {
        # an ipv4 family to negotiate ADD-PATH for, may be repeated
        ipv4 unicast|multicast|nlri-mpls|labeled-unicast|mpls-vpn|mcast-vpn|
                flow|flow-vpn|mup|sr-policy|rtc [limit <n>];
        # an ipv6 family to negotiate ADD-PATH for, may be repeated
        ipv6 unicast|multicast|nlri-mpls|labeled-unicast|mpls-vpn|mcast-vpn|
                mup|sr-policy|flow|flow-vpn [limit <n>];
        # an l2vpn family to negotiate ADD-PATH for, may be repeated
        l2vpn vpls|evpn [limit <n>];
        # an bgp-ls family to negotiate ADD-PATH for, may be repeated
        bgp-ls bgp-ls|bgp-ls-vpn [limit <n>];
        all;  # no family, ADD-PATH is negotiated for none, may be repeated
    }
    # the families whose next-hop may be of the other address family (RFC
    # 8950)
    nexthop {
        # an ipv4 family whose next-hop may be of the other address family,
        # may be repeated
        ipv4 unicast|multicast|nlri-mpls|labeled-unicast|mpls-vpn ipv6;
        # an ipv6 family whose next-hop may be of the other address family,
        # may be repeated
        ipv6 unicast|multicast|nlri-mpls|labeled-unicast|mpls-vpn ipv4;
    }
    # which API programs hear about this neighbor, and what they hear
    api [<name>] {
        # the programs, by name, which hear about the neighbor
        processes [ <process> ... ];
        # the programs whose name matches one of these regular expressions
        processes-match [ <regex> ... ];
        # tell the programs when the session goes up or down
        neighbor-changes true|false;
        # tell the programs what the OPEN messages negotiated
        negotiated true|false;
        fsm true|false;  # tell the programs each change of the state machine
        # tell the programs about the signals exabgp receives
        signal true|false;
        send {  # the messages sent which are given to the program
            parsed true|false;  # the messages decoded
            packets true|false;  # the messages as their bytes
            # the decoded and raw forms of a message together
            consolidate true|false;
            open true|false;  # the OPEN messages
            update true|false;  # the UPDATE messages
            notification true|false;  # the NOTIFICATION messages
            keepalive true|false;  # the KEEPALIVE messages
            refresh true|false;  # the ROUTE-REFRESH messages
            operational true|false;  # the OPERATIONAL messages
        }
        receive {  # the messages received which are given to the program
            parsed true|false;  # the messages decoded
            packets true|false;  # the messages as their bytes
            # the decoded and raw forms of a message together
            consolidate true|false;
            open true|false;  # the OPEN messages
            update true|false;  # the UPDATE messages
            notification true|false;  # the NOTIFICATION messages
            keepalive true|false;  # the KEEPALIVE messages
            refresh true|false;  # the ROUTE-REFRESH messages
            operational true|false;  # the OPERATIONAL messages
        }
    }
    static {  # routes to announce
        # a route, on one line, may be repeated
        route <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
        # the same attributes for several prefixes, may be repeated
        attributes <attribute> <value> ... nlri <prefix> ...;
        # the same attributes for several prefixes, as attributes, may be
        # repeated
        attribute <attribute> <value> ... nlri <prefix> ...;
        route <prefix> {  # a route, its values one per statement
            # the next-hop, or self for the local address, may be repeated
            next-hop <ip>|self;
            # the ADD-PATH path identifier, may be repeated
            path-information <number>|<ipv4>;
            # the route distinguisher, making it a VPN route, may be repeated
            rd <asn>:<n>|<ipv4>:<n>;
            route-distinguisher <asn>:<n>|<ipv4>:<n>;  # may be repeated
            # the MPLS label stack, may be repeated
            label <label>|[ <label> ... ];
            # may be repeated
            bgp-prefix-sid [ <label-index> ] | [ <label-index>, [ (
                    <base>,<range> ) ... ] ];
            # may be repeated
            bgp-prefix-sid-srv6 ( l3-service|l2-service <ipv6> [<behavior> [ [
                    <LBL>, <LNL>, <FL>, <AL>, <len>, <offset> ] ]] );
            # any attribute, as its wire bytes, may be repeated
            attribute [ 0x<code> 0x<flag> 0x<data> ];
            origin igp|egp|incomplete;  # may be repeated
            # RFC 9234 Only-to-Customer, may be repeated
            otc <asn>|self|<role>;
            med <0-4294967295>;  # may be repeated
            # may be repeated
            as-path <asn>|[ <asn> ... ] ( <asn> ... ) confed-sequence [ ... ]
                    confed-set [ ... ];
            local-preference <0-4294967295>;  # may be repeated
            atomic-aggregate;  # may be repeated
            aggregator ( <asn>:<router-id> );  # may be repeated
            originator-id <ipv4>;  # may be repeated
            cluster-list <ipv4>|[ <ipv4> ... ];  # may be repeated
            community <asn>:<value>|[ <asn>:<value> ... ];  # may be repeated
            # may be repeated
            large-community <asn>:<value>:<value>|[ <asn>:<value>:<value> ...
                    ];
            # may be repeated
            extended-community <type>:<value>|[ <type>:<value> ... ];
            aigp <number>|0x<hex>;  # may be repeated
            # a name for the route, kept by exabgp, may be repeated
            name <name>;
            # announce the prefix as its more specifics of this length, may be
            # repeated
            split /<length>;
            # the watchdog which announces and withdraws the route, may be
            # repeated
            watchdog <name>;
            withdraw;  # start with the route withdrawn, may be repeated
        }
        rtc ...;  # a route target membership route, RFC 4684, may be repeated
        # an SR policy route, may be repeated
        sr-policy distinguisher <n> color <n> endpoint <ip> next-hop <ip>
                [<sub-tlv> ...];
    }
    announce {  # routes by address family
        ipv4 {  # the ipv4 routes, by subsequent address family
            # a ipv4 unicast route, may be repeated
            unicast <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
            # a ipv4 multicast route, may be repeated
            multicast <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
            # a ipv4 nlri-mpls route, may be repeated
            nlri-mpls <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
            # a ipv4 mpls-vpn route, may be repeated
            mpls-vpn <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
            rtc ...;  # a ipv4 rtc route, may be repeated
            # a ipv4 flow rule, RFC 8955, may be repeated
            flow <match> <value> ... <action> <value> ...;
            # a ipv4 flow-vpn rule, RFC 8955, may be repeated
            flow-vpn <match> <value> ... <action> <value> ...;
            # a Mobile User Plane route, draft-mpmz-bess-mup-safi, may be
            # repeated
            mup mup-isd|mup-dsd|mup-t1st|mup-t2st ...;
            # a multicast VPN route, RFC 6514, may be repeated
            mcast-vpn source-ad|source-join|shared-join ...;
            # an SR policy route, RFC 9830, may be repeated
            sr-policy distinguisher <n> color <n> endpoint <ip> next-hop <ip>
                    [<sub-tlv> ...];
            labeled-unicast;
        }
        ipv6 {  # the ipv6 routes, by subsequent address family
            # a ipv6 unicast route, may be repeated
            unicast <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
            # a ipv6 multicast route, may be repeated
            multicast <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
            # a ipv6 nlri-mpls route, may be repeated
            nlri-mpls <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
            # a ipv6 mpls-vpn route, may be repeated
            mpls-vpn <prefix> next-hop <ip>|self [<attribute> <value> ...];
            # a ipv6 flow rule, RFC 8955, may be repeated
            flow <match> <value> ... <action> <value> ...;
            # a ipv6 flow-vpn rule, RFC 8955, may be repeated
            flow-vpn <match> <value> ... <action> <value> ...;
            # a Mobile User Plane route, draft-mpmz-bess-mup-safi, may be
            # repeated
            mup mup-isd|mup-dsd|mup-t1st|mup-t2st ...;
            # a multicast VPN route, RFC 6514, may be repeated
            mcast-vpn source-ad|source-join|shared-join ...;
            # an SR policy route, RFC 9830, may be repeated
            sr-policy distinguisher <n> color <n> endpoint <ip> next-hop <ip>
                    [<sub-tlv> ...];
            labeled-unicast;
        }
        l2vpn {  # the l2vpn routes
            # a VPLS route, RFC 4761, may be repeated
            vpls endpoint <n> base <n> offset <n> size <n> rd <rd> next-hop
                    <ip> [...];
        }
    }
    flow {  # FlowSpec routes (RFC 8955, RFC 8956)
        route <match> <value> ... <action> <value> ...;  # may be repeated
        route [<name>] {  # a flow route, what it matches and what it does
            match {  # what the route matches
                # the source prefix, RFC 8955 type 2, may be repeated
                source <ip>/<mask>[/<offset>];
                # the source prefix, as source, may be repeated
                source-ipv4 <ip>/<mask>[/<offset>];
                # the source prefix, as source, may be repeated
                source-ipv6 <ip>/<mask>[/<offset>];
                # the destination prefix, RFC 8955 type 1, may be repeated
                destination <ip>/<mask>[/<offset>];
                # the destination prefix, as destination, may be repeated
                destination-ipv4 <ip>/<mask>[/<offset>];
                # the destination prefix, as destination, may be repeated
                destination-ipv6 <ip>/<mask>[/<offset>];
                # the IP protocol, RFC 8955 type 3, may be repeated
                protocol <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the IPv6 next header, RFC 8956 type 3, may be repeated
                next-header <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the source or destination port, RFC 8955 type 4, may be
                # repeated
                port <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the destination port, RFC 8955 type 5, may be repeated
                destination-port <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the source port, RFC 8955 type 6, may be repeated
                source-port <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the ICMP type, RFC 8955 type 7, may be repeated
                icmp-type <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the ICMP code, RFC 8955 type 8, may be repeated
                icmp-code <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the TCP flags, RFC 8955 type 9, may be repeated
                tcp-flags <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the packet length, RFC 8955 type 10, may be repeated
                packet-length <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the DSCP, RFC 8955 type 11, may be repeated
                dscp <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the IPv6 traffic class, RFC 8956 type 11, may be repeated
                traffic-class <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the fragment flags, RFC 8955 type 12, may be repeated
                fragment <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
                # the IPv6 flow label, RFC 8956 type 13, may be repeated
                flow-label <op><value>[&...] | [ ... ];
            }
            then {  # what is done with what matches
                accept;  # no action: the traffic is accepted, may be repeated
                # drop the traffic, a traffic-rate of 0, may be repeated
                discard;
                # traffic-rate, RFC 8955 7.3: bytes or packets per second, may
                # be repeated
                rate-limit <number> [bytes|packets];
                # redirect to the VRF of a route target, or to an address, may
                # be repeated
                redirect <asn>:<nn>|<ip>|[<ipv6>]:<nn>;
                # redirect to the next-hop of the route, or to the address
                # given, may be repeated
                redirect-to-nexthop [<ip>];
                # redirect to an address, the IETF community, may be repeated
                redirect-to-nexthop-ietf <ip>;
                # redirect to the next-hop of the UPDATE, the older form, may
                # be repeated
                redirect-to-nexthop-simpson;
                # copy the traffic to an address, the IETF community, may be
                # repeated
                copy <ip>;
                # copy the traffic to an address, the older form, may be
                # repeated
                copy-simpson <ip>;
                # redirect to an address, the older form, may be repeated
                redirect-simpson <ip>;
                # traffic-marking, RFC 8955 7.5: the DSCP to set, may be
                # repeated
                mark <0-63>;
                # traffic-action, RFC 8955 7.6: sample the traffic, stop at
                # this rule, or both, may be repeated
                action sample|terminal|sample-terminal;
                # may be repeated
                community <asn>:<value>|[ <asn>:<value> ... ];
                # may be repeated
                large-community <asn>:<value>:<value>|[ <asn>:<value>:<value>
                        ... ];
                # may be repeated
                extended-community <type>:<value>|[ <type>:<value> ... ];
            }
            scope {  # where the route applies
                # the interfaces the rule applies to, draft-ietf-idr-flowspec-
                # interfaceset, may be repeated
                interface-set <transitive>:<direction>:<asn>:<group>;
            }
            rd <asn>:<n>|<ipv4>:<n>;  # may be repeated
            route-distinguisher <asn>:<n>|<ipv4>:<n>;  # may be repeated
            path-information <number>|<ipv4>;  # may be repeated
            # the next-hop of the flow route, or self, may be repeated
            next-hop <ip>|self;
        }
    }
    l2vpn {  # VPLS routes
        # a VPLS route, on one line, may be repeated
        vpls endpoint <n> base <n> offset <n> size <n> rd <rd> next-hop <ip>
                [...];
        vpls [<name>] {  # a VPLS route, its values one per statement
            # the next-hop, or self for the IPv4 local address, may be
            # repeated
            next-hop <ip>|self;
            rd <asn>:<n>|<ipv4>:<n>;  # may be repeated
            endpoint <0-65535>;  # the VE ID of the site, may be repeated
            offset <0-65535>;  # the VE block offset, may be repeated
            size <0-65535>;  # the VE block size, may be repeated
            base <0-1048575>;  # the label base, may be repeated
            attribute [ 0x<code> 0x<flag> 0x<data> ];  # may be repeated
            origin igp|egp|incomplete;  # may be repeated
            med <0-4294967295>;  # may be repeated
            # may be repeated
            as-path <asn>|[ <asn> ... ] ( <asn> ... ) confed-sequence [ ... ]
                    confed-set [ ... ];
            local-preference <0-4294967295>;  # may be repeated
            atomic-aggregate;  # may be repeated
            aggregator ( <asn>:<router-id> );  # may be repeated
            originator-id <ipv4>;  # may be repeated
            cluster-list <ipv4>|[ <ipv4> ... ];  # may be repeated
            community <asn>:<value>|[ <asn>:<value> ... ];  # may be repeated
            # may be repeated
            extended-community <type>:<value>|[ <type>:<value> ... ];
            name <name>;  # may be repeated
            split /<length>;  # may be repeated
            watchdog <name>;  # may be repeated
            withdraw;  # may be repeated
        }
        next-hop;
        rd;
        endpoint;
        offset;
        size;
        base;
        attribute [ 0x<code> 0x<flag> 0x<data> ];  # may be repeated
        origin igp|egp|incomplete;  # may be repeated
        med <0-4294967295>;  # may be repeated
        # may be repeated
        as-path <asn>|[ <asn> ... ] ( <asn> ... ) confed-sequence [ ... ]
                confed-set [ ... ];
        local-preference <0-4294967295>;  # may be repeated
        atomic-aggregate;  # may be repeated
        aggregator ( <asn>:<router-id> );  # may be repeated
        originator-id <ipv4>;  # may be repeated
        cluster-list <ipv4>|[ <ipv4> ... ];  # may be repeated
        community <asn>:<value>|[ <asn>:<value> ... ];  # may be repeated
        # may be repeated
        extended-community <type>:<value>|[ <type>:<value> ... ];
        name <name>;  # may be repeated
        split /<length>;  # may be repeated
        watchdog <name>;  # may be repeated
        withdraw;  # may be repeated
    }
    operational {  # the operational messages sent to the peer
        # Advisory State Message, may be repeated
        asm afi <afi> safi <safi> advisory <advisory>;
        # Advisory Dump Message, may be repeated
        adm afi <afi> safi <safi> advisory <advisory>;
        # Reachable Prefix Count Query, may be repeated
        rpcq afi <afi> safi <safi> sequence <sequence>;
        # Reachable Prefix Count Reply, may be repeated
        rpcp afi <afi> safi <safi> sequence <sequence> counter <counter>;
        # Adj-RIB-Out Prefix Count Query, may be repeated
        apcq afi <afi> safi <safi> sequence <sequence>;
        # Adj-RIB-Out Prefix Count Reply, may be repeated
        apcp afi <afi> safi <safi> sequence <sequence> counter <counter>;
        # Local Prefix Count Query, may be repeated
        lpcq afi <afi> safi <safi> sequence <sequence>;
        # Local Prefix Count Reply, may be repeated
        lpcp afi <afi> safi <safi> sequence <sequence> counter <counter>;
    }
}
template {  # statements shared by neighbors
    # a template, the statements of a neighbor which inherits i ... }  # as
    # neighbor
    neighbor <name> {
}
.\" syntax: end
.Ed
.Pp
When a process exits,
.Cm on-exit withdraw
withdraws the routes it announced from every neighbor, and a route which
replaced one from the configuration gives the configured one back.
This happens even when the process is respawned: the new process starts
with nothing announced.
.Cm on-exit keep
leaves the routes announced, as releases before 6.0 did.
.Pp
Without
.Cm on-exit ,
the API version of the process decides: the routes of a process using
API 4, the commands of releases before 6.0, are kept, and those of any
other process are withdrawn.
.Nm exabgp migrate conf
writes
.Cm on-exit keep
in each process section it migrates.
.Pp
Please note that multi-line sections really do need to be written as
multiple lines: the first one ending in "{", and ending with a line
with a "}" by itself.
.Sh ONLY TO CUSTOMER
The optional role block names our local eBGP relationship, not the remote role.
It requires explicit, unequal local-as and peer-as values.
Strict mode rejects a peer that omits the Role capability; otherwise its
complementary role is inferred. Incompatible advertised roles reject the OPEN.
.Pp
Static routes and API announcements accept an OTC ASN in decimal or dotted form.
The attribute always carries four octets, even with a two-octet-ASN peer.
For example, "otc 1.1" is emitted and reported as ASN 65537.
The "otc self" form resolves the announcing session's effective local ASN,
including local-as auto, without modifying the stored route.
A role name resolves the same ASN and additionally asserts that the neighbor's
configured local role matches it; a missing or different role is an error.
.Pp
Without a route-level OTC instruction, role-based marking adds the local ASN
when sending IPv4 or IPv6 unicast to a customer, peer, or route-server client.
There is no way to turn that off. RFC 9234 section 5 says the operator MUST NOT
have the ability to modify these procedures, so the "role otc" sub-option and the
route-level "otc none" instruction were removed in 6.0.0; a configuration using
either is refused with a message naming the RFC. Other address families are
untouched, as the same section requires. Existing explicit OTC values are
preserved.
.Pp
A route already carrying OTC is refused toward a provider, peer, or route
server. This applies to explicit "otc self" and role-named forms as well.
Replacing an advertised route with a refused route withdraws the old advertisement.
Withdrawals are never refused. Non-unicast families bypass these policy rules,
while preserving explicit OTC.
.Pp
Inline "exabgp encode" accepts a numeric OTC. Resolving "self"
or a role name requires "exabgp encode -c" with a real configuration.
For "otc self", offline encoding with "local-as auto" requires a concrete
"peer-as"; if both are automatic, encoding reports a configuration error.
Named OTC appears as a numeric "otc" member in decoded attribute JSON.
.Pp
Role and strict-mode changes restart the session. Changing add-meta does not.
.Pp
Received unicast leaks are logged and annotated on affected announcement entries
under "meta.route-leak". Disabling add-meta hides the annotation, not the warning
or outbound refusal. Malformed OTC uses RFC 7606 treat-as-withdraw.
Leak and export-refusal warnings use the normally enabled reactor logging category;
parser and RIB debug logging need not be enabled. Global logging and level filters
still apply.
This is partial RFC 9234 support: ExaBGP does not insert missing ingress OTC.
Helpers redistributing received routes must perform ingress marking and exclude
ineligible routes. The receive and send/receive marking settings are rejected.

.Sh GENERALIZED TTL SECURITY
GTSM (RFC 5082) is two settings on a session socket, and
.Nm
has one keyword for each.
.Ic incoming-ttl
is the lowest TTL the kernel will accept from the peer, so a packet forged
from off-link arrives with a TTL too low and is dropped before
.Xr exabgp 1
sees it.
.Ic outgoing-ttl
is the TTL we send with, and it also serves as the multihop setting it
predates.
.Pp
Both now apply to a session whichever end opened it.
Until 6.0.0 a session this side initiated installed
.Ic outgoing-ttl
and no inbound check at all, and a session the peer opened inherited a
sending TTL equal to the minimum we accept, where section 3 of the RFC has a
GTSM sender use 255.
An accepted connection takes the sending TTL of the neighbor it matched:
.Ic outgoing-ttl
when it is configured, else 255 when
.Ic incoming-ttl
is, else the kernel default.
For the usual
.Dq incoming-ttl 255
that is what passive sessions already sent, so a working GTSM pair is
unaffected.
.Pp
The inbound check needs a per-platform socket option.
It is installed on Linux and FreeBSD for both IPv4 and IPv6.
macOS has no IPv4 equivalent, so
.Nm
warns and brings the session up with the outbound TTL only: on that platform
IPv4
.Ic incoming-ttl
is not enforced.
.Pp
The listening socket is shared by every neighbor on one address and port, so
neighbors behind it configured with different
.Ic incoming-ttl
values get whichever was set last.
Give a neighbor needing its own minimum its own listening address.
.Sh CONFEDERATION
With a
.Ic confederation
block the neighbor is a member of an RFC 5065 confederation.
.Ic local-as
is our Member-AS Number and
.Ic identifier
the AS the world outside sees.
A peer whose
.Ic peer-as
is our own or one of the
.Ic members
is inside the confederation, any other is outside it.
.Pp
The OPEN carries the identifier to a peer outside and our Member-AS to a
peer inside.
A route with no as-path is sent with the identifier in an AS_SEQUENCE to a
peer outside, our Member-AS in an AS_CONFED_SEQUENCE to another member, and
an empty path inside our own Member-AS.
LOCAL_PREF goes to every peer inside the confederation.
.Pp
A configured as-path is sent as written, as it is outside a confederation,
so a route for another member should start with
.Dq confed-sequence [ <local-as> ] .
The confederation segments are removed from any path sent to a peer outside.
On receipt, confederation segments from a peer outside, or a path from
another member not starting with an AS_CONFED_SEQUENCE, are treated as
withdrawn.
Without a confederation block these paths are accepted as before.
.Sh AS_SET AND AS_CONFED_SET
RFC 9774 deprecates both path segment types.
A route received with either in its AS_PATH, or in an AS4_PATH from a peer
without four octet AS support, is treated as withdrawn, as RFC 7606 describes.
.Dq as-set accept
on a neighbor passes such routes on unchanged, for a route collector which
wants to see them.
.Pp
Routes we send are not checked: a set only reaches the wire when a route was
written with one, and the RFC leaves that to the operator.
.Sh TUNNEL ENCAPSULATION
RFC 9012 asks for the Tunnel Encapsulation attribute to be filtered from the
UPDATEs received on every EBGP session unless told otherwise.
.Dq tunnel-encapsulation auto ,
the default, removes it from what an EBGP neighbor sends, before it is decoded,
and keeps it from a neighbor inside the AS or in another Member-AS of the
confederation.
.Dq tunnel-encapsulation filter
removes it on any session, and
.Dq tunnel-encapsulation accept
keeps it on any session.
The route itself is accepted either way.
.Sh GRACEFUL RESTART, RECEIVING
When the TCP session of a neighbor which advertised Graceful Restart is lost,
the routes it sent are kept in the adj-rib-in, marked stale (RFC 4724).
They are removed, and the API told as for a withdrawal, when the neighbor's
End-of-RIB for their family arrives, when its next OPEN no longer offers
to keep forwarding for the family, or when its Restart Time passes with no
new session.
A NOTIFICATION ends the session without keeping anything.
It needs
.Dq adj-rib-in .
.Sh ROUTE TARGET CONSTRAINT
With the rtc family negotiated, exabgp announces and withdraws Route Target
membership and decodes what the peer sends, but by default sends every VPN
route whatever that membership, which RFC 4684 permits.
.Dq route-target-filter true
sends an IPv4 or IPv6 VPN or EVPN route only when one of its Route Targets is
covered by the membership the peer advertised, and offers the routes again
whenever that membership changes, withdrawing those no longer covered.
It needs
.Dq adj-rib-in
and
.Dq adj-rib-out ,
both on by default.
.Sh FIRST AS OF AN EXTERNAL ROUTE
A route received from an EBGP neighbor outside the confederation whose AS_PATH
does not start with the neighbor's AS is treated as withdrawn, as RFC 8955
section 6 requires and RFC 4271 permits, and logged.
An internet exchange route server does not add its own AS to the paths it
passes on:
.Dq enforce-first-as false
on its neighbor turns the check off.
.Sh FLOW SPECIFICATION
A flow route is sent with no next hop in its MP_REACH_NLRI (RFC 8955), unless it
redirects to that next hop with the draft-simpson community
.Dq ( redirect-simpson ) .
A flow route matching an ICMP type or code together with a port can match no
packet, and is not announced; a warning names it.
A flow label is sent in four octets (RFC 8956), and the two high bits of a
received DSCP are ignored.
.Pp
.Dq flow-validation enable
applies the validation of RFC 8955 section 6 to the flow routes a neighbor
sends: one is kept only when the same neighbor announced the longest unicast
route covering its destination, from the same originator, with no more specific
route inside it from another neighbouring AS.
One which fails is not given to the API nor kept in the adj-rib-in, and is
announced to the API once a unicast route makes it valid; a flow route which
stops being valid is withdrawn from the API.
.Dq flow-validation relaxed
also accepts a flow route with no destination.
The default,
.Dq disable ,
checks nothing, which the RFC only allows by explicit configuration: it is
kept for this release because many flow routes arrive with no unicast route.
It needs
.Dq adj-rib-in .
.Sh MULTIPLE LABELS
RFC 8277 lets a labelled unicast or VPN route (SAFI 4 or 128) carry more than
one label only when both speakers sent the Multiple Labels Capability, and never
more than the number the peer said it can take.
exabgp does not send the capability unless
.Dq capability { multiple-labels <count>; }
asks for it, with the number of labels it accepts on one prefix.
Where the capability did not go both ways, a route configured with
.Dq label [ 100 200 ]
is sent with its first label only; where it did, the stack is cut to the
number the peer announced.
.Sh RE-ADVERTISED ROUTES
A route decoded from one peer and handed to another neighbor (only a test or an
embedding program does this: the API re-reads text, which makes a route of our
own) is re-advertised in the sense of RFC 1997, RFC 4360 and RFC 7911. It is not
sent past the boundary a NO_EXPORT, NO_ADVERTISE or NO_EXPORT_SUBCONFED
community draws, loses its non-transitive extended communities towards another
AS, and goes out under an ADD-PATH identifier exabgp chooses.
A route from the configuration or the API is ours and is sent as configured:
.Dq community no-export
towards a transit is left for the transit to act on.
.Sh ATTRIBUTES FROM AN EXTERNAL NEIGHBOR
As RFC 7606 requires, LOCAL_PREF, ORIGINATOR_ID and CLUSTER_LIST received from
an EBGP neighbor outside the confederation are discarded and the rest of the
UPDATE is processed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr exabgp 1 .
.Sh CAVEATS
Default values ought to be documented.
The functionality and semantics of the process / api ought to be documented.
The healthcheck module of exabgp is perhaps deserved of its own man page.
